---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title       = 'Australian Raven vs Little Raven — Identification Guide';
const description = 'Australian Raven and Little Raven overlap in south-eastern Australia. Key differences: throat hackles, wing-flicking, call, size, and habitat. How to tell them apart.';
const path        = '/compare/australian-raven-vs-little-raven/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',    url: '/' },
  { name: 'Compare', url: '/compare/' },
  { name: 'Australian Raven vs Little Raven', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'How do you tell an Australian Raven from a Little Raven?', a: 'Key identification features: (1) Australian Raven has very prominent elongated throat hackles; Little Raven has shorter, less prominent hackles. (2) Little Raven wing-flicks on landing — a reliable behavioural feature. (3) Call: Australian Raven gives a distinctive wailing, descending "aah-aah-aah" ending in a mournful droop; Little Raven gives a shorter, more clipped, faster call. (4) Size: Australian Raven is slightly larger (46–53 cm vs 48–50 cm for Little Raven — overlap considerable). (5) Iris: both have white iris as adults.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} ogType="article"
  datePublished="2026-11-07" dateModified="2026-11-07"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} faq={faq} pageType="Article">

<article class="id-page">
  <nav class="id-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol>
      {breadcrumbs.map((b, i) => (
        <li>
          {i < breadcrumbs.length - 1
            ? <><a href={b.url}>{b.name}</a><span aria-hidden="true"> › </span></>
            : <span aria-current="page">{b.name}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>
  <header class="id-header">
    <p class="id-pillar">Compare · Australian Corvids</p>
    <h1>Australian Raven vs Little Raven</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle"><em>Corvus coronoides</em> vs <em>Corvus mellori</em></p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Both species are large all-black corvids that overlap in south-eastern Australia. The best features: <strong>Australian Raven has very prominent long throat hackles</strong> and a wailing descending call; <strong>Little Raven wing-flicks on landing</strong> and has a faster, less mournful call. Both have white iris as adults. Size overlap is too great to rely on alone.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>At a Glance</h2>
    <div class="id-compare-table-wrap">
      <table class="id-compare-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Australian Raven</th><th>Little Raven</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Length</td><td>46–53 cm</td><td>48–50 cm</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Throat hackles</td><td>Very prominent — long, shaggy</td><td>Present but shorter; less prominent</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Eye colour</td><td>White iris (adult)</td><td>White iris (adult)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wing-flicking</td><td>Not characteristic</td><td>Flicks wings on landing — diagnostic</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Call</td><td>Wailing, long, descending at end</td><td>Faster, shorter, more clipped — less mournful</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tail</td><td>Fan-shaped</td><td>Fan-shaped (shorter than Australian Raven proportionally)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Habitat</td><td>Widespread; cities, woodland, coast, outback</td><td>SE Australia; more montane and nomadic; large winter flocks</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Range</td><td>All Australia except Tasmania</td><td>Victoria, SE SA, SE NSW, SW WA</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Throat Hackles</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Both species have throat hackles (elongated pointed feathers on the throat and upper chest), but:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Australian Raven:</strong> Hackles are very prominent, long, and shaggy — they droop conspicuously forward when the bird calls</li>
      <li><strong>Little Raven:</strong> Hackles present but shorter and less prominent; the bird has a "neater" throat appearance</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When an Australian Raven throws its head back and calls, the flowing hackles are immediately distinctive. This feature is reliable but requires a reasonable view.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Wing-Flicking — The Little Raven's Diagnostic Habit</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Little Ravens have a habit of <strong>flicking both wings over the back simultaneously on landing</strong> — a quick, repeated gesture performed several times after alighting. This habit is:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Characteristic and consistent in Little Ravens</li>
      <li>Not performed by Australian Ravens (or other Australian corvids consistently)</li>
      <li>Visible at reasonable distance</li>
      <li>The single most useful behavioural identification feature for the two species</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Once seen, the wing-flick is unmistakable. It is worth watching any perched crow-like bird in SE Australia for this behaviour when corvid identification is uncertain.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Call</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Australian Raven:</strong> A distinctive wailing call — often described as "aah-aah-aaaah" or "oaah-oaah-aaaah," long in duration, with the last syllable drawn out in a mournful descending wail. Unmistakable once heard.</li>
      <li><strong>Little Raven:</strong> A faster, shorter, more clipped series of calls — not mournful or wailing; similar pattern to Australian Raven but quicker and lacking the characteristic descending end. Also a harsh "korrk" or "karrk."</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Call is very reliable for experienced observers. The Australian Raven's distinctive wail is one of the most characteristic sounds in Australian bird life.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Range and Habitat</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Australian Raven:</strong> The most widespread Australian corvid — found throughout mainland Australia but absent from Tasmania; occupies a huge range of habitats including urban centres of all major mainland cities</li>
      <li><strong>Little Raven:</strong> Restricted to south-eastern Australia (Victoria, SE South Australia, SE NSW, SW Western Australia); more nomadic; forms large winter flocks in agricultural areas; more common at altitude than Australian Raven in some areas</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Outside south-eastern Australia, any large Australian corvid is likely an Australian Raven. In south-eastern Victoria and suburban Melbourne, both species may be present and the identification features above are essential.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-faq id-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <h2 id="faq-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    {faq.map(item => (
      <details class="id-faq-item">
        <summary class="id-faq-q">{item.q}</summary>
        <p class="id-faq-a">{item.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="id-sources id-section" aria-labelledby="src-h2">
    <h2 id="src-h2">Sources</h2>
    <ol class="id-src-list">
      <li id="src-1">Madge, S. &amp; Burn, H. (1994). <em>Crows and Jays</em>. Christopher Helm. — species accounts for Australian Raven and Little Raven; identification features.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="id-claim-note"><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> field-guide data</p>
  </section>

  <nav class="id-nav-bottom">
    <a href="/species/australian-raven/identification/" class="id-back">← Australian Raven ID</a>
    <a href="/species/little-raven/identification/" class="id-next">Little Raven ID →</a>
  </nav>
</article>
</BaseLayout>

